==Initial adaptations==
===Early films===
{{Main|Batman (serial)|l1=''Batman'' (serial)|Batman and Robin (serial)|l2=''Batman and Robin'' (serial)|Batman (1966 film)|l3=''Batman'' (1966 film)}}
[[File:Adam West Batman 1966.jpg|thumb|upright|Publicity shot of Adam West in costume, 1966]]

[[Batman]] is the superhero persona of industrialist Bruce Wayne, a character created by [[Bob Kane]] and [[Bill Finger]] for comic books published by [[DC Comics]]{{efn|At the time, DC was known as Detective Comics, one of at least two [[imprint (trade name)|imprints]] of [[National Comics Publications]].{{sfnm|1a1=Conner|1y=2023|1p=5|2a1=Daniels|2y=1999|2p=21}}}} in 1939.{{sfn|Daniels|1999|p=20}} On the strength of the earliest comics, three major Hollywood studios approached DC to purchase the film rights in the early 1940s.{{sfn|Brooker|2001|pp=80–81}} [[Columbia Pictures]] bought the rights shortly afterward, and, in 1943, released a fifteen-chapter [[serial film]] directed by [[Lambert Hillyer]] and starring [[Lewis Wilson]] as Batman.{{sfnm|1a1=Mayer|1y=2017|1pp=45–47|2a1=Conner|2y=2023|2pp=5–6|3a1=Dick|3y=1993|3p=164}} The story follows Batman and Robin's attempts to sabotage a Japanese spy's plot to aid [[Axis powers|Axis]] conquest of the United States by producing a super weapon.{{sfnm|1a1=Kinnard|1y=2008|1p=103|2a1=Rainey|2y=2010|2pp=22–23}} Elements of ''Batman'' deviate from the source material in significant respects, most notably the addition of a Japanese villain, underscoring the film's [[Propaganda in World War II|propaganda function]].{{sfnm|1a1=Mayer|1y=2017|1pp=45–47|2a1=Brooker|2y=2001|2pp=84–85}} Conceived eighteen months after [[attack on Pearl Harbor|Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor]] in December 1941, ''Batman'' reflected a wider cultural shift to arouse mass support for US intervention in [[World War II]].{{sfnm|1a1=Mayer|1y=2017|1pp=45–47|2a1=Biesen|2y=2005|2p=4}} Nevertheless, the serial introduced the [[Batcave]] and the [[Wayne Manor]]'s secret [[grandfather clock]] entrance in Batman mythology.{{sfn|Daniels|1999|pp=57–59}} It was re-released in theaters in 1965 under the title ''An Evening with Batman and Robin''.{{sfn|Mayer|2017|pp=45–47}}

In 1949, Columbia developed another fifteen-part serial, ''Batman and Robin'', as the sequel to ''Batman'', compelled by the success of ''[[Superman (serial)|Superman]]'' the previous year.{{sfn|Mayer|2017|pp=45–47}} [[Robert Lowery (actor)|Robert Lowery]] was Wilson's replacement as Batman, leading a new ensemble of actors opposite [[Johnny Duncan (actor)|Johnny Duncan]] as Robin.{{sfnm|1a1=Smith|1y=2013|1p=76–77|2a1=Rainey|2y=2010|2pp=22–23}} ''Batman and Robin'' details the duo's retrieval of a stolen remote control machine from criminal mastermind [[Wizard (DC Comics)|Wizard]], whose schemes threaten to disrupt Gotham City's transportation networks.{{sfnm|1a1=Mayer|1y=2017|1pp=45–47|2a1=Rainey|2y=2010|2pp=22–23}} Producer [[Sam Katzman]] sought to keep the cost of filming low, and the diminished budget led to further changes to the onscreen world.{{sfn|Mayer|2017|pp=45–47}} As a consequence, ''Batman and Robin'' fared poorly in reviews from the press.<ref name=smith>{{harvnb|Smith|2013|p=76–77}}</ref>

The [[American Broadcasting Company]]'s (ABC) creation of ''[[Batman (TV series)|Batman]]'', a live-action TV adaptation running in the mid-1960s, resurrected the character's popularity.<ref name=smith/> [[20th Century Fox]] released a theatrical film, also titled ''[[Batman (1966 film)|Batman]]'', intended to be a precursor to the show in 1966.{{sfn|Shubilla|2022|p=20}} Featuring [[Adam West]] as the superhero, author Matthew J. Smith viewed the projects as [[camp (style)|campy]], yet more faithful interpretations of the comics than the serials.<ref name=smith/> West stated he was hesitant to consider the part because he feared being [[typecast]], but was convinced by his agent.{{sfn|West|1994|pp=53–54}} In the film, Batman and Robin ([[Burt Ward]]) confront the main antagonists of the series: the [[Joker (character)|Joker]] ([[Cesar Romero]]), the [[Penguin (character)|Penguin]] ([[Burgess Meredith]]), the [[Riddler]] ([[Frank Gorshin]]), and [[Catwoman]] ([[Lee Meriwether]]).{{sfn|Reinhart|2013|p=193}}

===Limbo===

ABC's cancellation of ''Batman'' decreased Hollywood interest in further film adaptations.<ref name=smith/> A concerted effort to produce another film did not begin until a year after the release of ''[[Superman (1978 film)|Superman]]'' (1978).{{sfn|Hughes|2007|p=}} Producers [[Michael Uslan]] and [[Benjamin Melniker]] purchased the rights with the aim of conceiving a more faithful adaptation of the comics.<ref name=CBS>{{cite news|first = Bill|last = Ramey|url = http://www.batman-on-film.com/interview_muslan_2.html|title = An Interview With Michael Uslan – Part 1|work = Batman-on-Film|date = November 8, 2005|access-date = May 4, 2008|archive-date = September 27, 2013|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130927101837/http://www.batman-on-film.com/interview_muslan_2.html|url-status = dead}}</ref> ''Superman'' was another catalyst for Uslan and Melniker's vision of a Batman film.{{sfn|Schatz|2018|p=113}} Uslan [[pitch (filmmaking)|pitched]] to multiple studios unsuccessfully, including Columbia and [[United Artists]], prompting him to devise an outline, ''Return of the Batman'', to better articulate his idea.<ref name=CBS/><ref name=Columbia>{{cite news|first = Bill|last = Ramey|url = http://www.batman-on-film.com/interview_muslan_3.html|title = An Interview With Michael Uslan – Part 2|work = Batman-on-Film|date = November 11, 2005|access-date = May 4, 2008|archive-date = September 27, 2013|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130927101835/http://www.batman-on-film.com/interview_muslan_3.html|url-status = dead}}</ref>

By November 1979, Uslan and Melniker obtained funding through a joint venture with [[Peter Guber]], chairman of the film division of [[Casablanca Records]].{{sfnm|1a1=Schatz|1y=2018|1p=113|2a1=Griffin|2a2=Masters|2y=1997|2pp=164}} Under the arrangement, the producers were entitled to 40 percent of profits yielded by Casablanca.{{sfn|Griffin|Masters|1997|p=165–166}} They commissioned a Batman film with a $15 million budget in 1981, but a series of corporate acquisitions prolonged negotiations over the film's distribution, stalling development.<ref name=cine1/>{{sfn|Uslan|2019|p=193}} Casablanca's preexisting distribution agreement with [[Universal Pictures]] dissolved after the company was acquired by [[PolyGram Filmed Entertainment|PolyGram Pictures]].{{sfn|Griffin|Masters|1997|p=165–166}} By this point, PolyGram faced bankruptcy after investing $80 million to increase their rate of output, and Guber brokered an agreement to transfer ownership of the Batman film rights to him and associate [[Jon Peters]].<ref name=griffin2>{{harvnb|Griffin|Masters|1997|pp=113–114}}</ref> Another associate pitched the project to [[Warner Bros. Pictures]] executive [[Frank Wells]], and Peters then signed a deal with studio president [[Terry Semel]] which overlapped with the Casablanca contract.<ref name=cine1>{{cite news|first = Alan|last = Jones|url = http://www.batman-online.com/index.php?articles;id=2|title = ''Batman'' in Production|publisher = [[Cinefantastique]]|pages = 75–88|date = November 1989|access-date = May 13, 2008|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131204224714/http://www.batman-online.com/index.php?articles;id=2|archive-date = December 4, 2013|url-status = dead}}</ref><ref name=griffin2/> Since Uslan and Melniker were unaware of the dealings, they sued Warner Bros. in 1992 claiming a [[breach of contract]] on grounds of coercion and fraud.{{sfn|Griffin|Masters|1997|p=165–166, 173}}

Screenwriter [[Tom Mankiewicz]] completed the project's first draft in June 1983, titled ''The Batman''.<ref name=ignthebatman1983/> The draft focused on an origin story chronicling Wayne's transformation into Batman.<ref name=ignthebatman1983/> Moreover, Mankiewicz developed the story to indicate a sequel following Batman and [[Dick Grayson]] as a crimefighting duo.<ref name=ignthebatman1983>{{cite news|author = Stax|title = The Stax Report Special Edition: Script Review of ''The Batman''|work = [[IGN]]|date = December 1, 2001|url = http://movies.ign.com/articles/036/036038p1.html|access-date =October 24, 2008| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20081206080334/http://movies.ign.com/articles/036/036038p1.html| archive-date= December 6, 2008 | url-status=live}}</ref> Mankiewicz took inspiration from ''Batman: Strange Apparitions'', a multi-issue [[limited series (comics)|limited series]] by [[Steve Englehart]] and [[Marshall Rogers]].<ref name=road>{{cite AV media|first1=Michael|last1=Uslan|author-link1=Michael Uslan|first2=Benjamin|last2=Melniker|author-link2=Benjamin Melniker|first3=Peter|last3=Guber|author-link3=Peter Guber|first4=Tom|last4=Mankiewicz|author-link4=Tom Mankiewicz|first5=Sam|last5=Hamm|author-link5=Sam Hamm|title=Shadows of the Bat: The Cinematic Saga of the Dark Knight—The Road to Gotham City|date=2005|publisher=[[Warner Home Video]]|medium=DVD}}</ref> Though ''The Batman'' was announced with a mid-1985 release date,<ref name=Jones>{{cite news|first = Alan|last = Jones|url = http://www.1989batman.com/2013/08/vintage-magazine-article.html|title = Batman|work = [[Cinefantastique]]|pages = 55–67|date = November 1989|access-date = May 2, 2008|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131205004312/http://www.1989batman.com/2013/08/vintage-magazine-article.html|archive-date = December 5, 2013|url-status = dead}}</ref> revisions to the script impeded progress on the film.{{sfn|Hanke|1999|p=20}} In total, the script underwent ten rewrites from ten screenwriters at the behest of several different directors.{{sfn|Hanke|1999|p=20, 75}}

==Original film series==
{{see also|Bruce Wayne (1989 film series character)}}

===''Batman'' (1989)===
{{main|Batman (1989 film) |l1=''Batman'' (1989 film)}}
[[File:Michael Keaton Cannes.jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Cropped photograph of Michael Keaton at the 55th Cannes Film Festival|Michael Keaton in 2002]]

Warner Bros. appointed [[Tim Burton]] as ''Batman'' director in 1988.{{sfn|Burton|2000|pp=70–71}} The studio approached Burton as early as 1985, but had no formalized deal until a week after ''[[Beetlejuice]]'' opened in theaters.{{sfn|Burton|2000|pp=70–71}} Burton discarded Mankiewicz's screenplay as he found the tone too similar to ''Superman''.<ref name=Jones/> Englehart and [[Julie Hickson]] prepared [[film treatment]]s which [[Sam Hamm]] reworked into the script.{{sfn|Burton|2000|pp=70–71}}<ref name="Steve">{{cite web|title= Batman|work= SteveEnglehart.com|url= http://www.steveenglehart.com/Film/Batman%20movie.html|access-date= November 25, 2007|first= Steve|last= Englehat|quote= So I got to do the second treatment with just the characters that eventually hit the screen: Bruce Wayne, the Batman, Silver St. Cloud, Boss Thorne, and the Joker. |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071214111803/http://www.steveenglehart.com/Film/Batman%20movie.html |archive-date= December 14, 2007|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Frank Miller]]'s ''[[The Dark Knight Returns]]'' (1986) guided Hamm's script, reflecting a darker approach than previous interpretations to that point.{{sfn|Smith|2013|p=77}} When the [[1988 Writers Guild of America strike]] forced Hamm to resign, Warner Bros. engaged [[Warren Skaaren]], [[Charles McKeown]] and [[Jonathan Gems]] for additional rewrites to lighten the tone.{{sfn|Hughes|2007|p=}} 

Burton chose [[Michael Keaton]] among a number of leading men as [[Bruce Wayne (1989 film series character)|Wayne]], despite resistance from studio executives.<ref name=Jones/><ref name=Busch>{{cite news|author=Busch, Jenna|url=http://www.superherohype.com/features/306949-exclusive-interview-batman-producer-michael-uslan-talks-the-legacy-of-superhero-cinema|title=Interview: Batman Producer Michael Uslan Talks the Legacy of Superhero Cinema|publisher=[[Mandatory (company)|SuperHeroHype]]|date=July 3, 2014|access-date=July 25, 2015|archive-date=December 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191207110523/https://www.superherohype.com/features/306949-exclusive-interview-batman-producer-michael-uslan-talks-the-legacy-of-superhero-cinema|url-status=live}}</ref> Keaton was a controversial casting choice; he had been mainly known as a comedic actor and was not perceived to fit the Batman archetype.{{sfn|Burton|2000|p=74}} [[Jack Nicholson]] stars opposite Keaton as the [[Joker (Jack Napier)|Joker]], earning as much as $50–$90 million for his work.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/article/2000/03/03/biggest-movie-payoffs/|title=The biggest movie payoffs|last=Hochman|first=David|date=March 3, 2000|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|access-date=January 4, 2026|archive-date=December 22, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222151019/https://ew.com/article/2000/03/03/biggest-movie-payoffs/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.cnn.com/2013/11/08/showbiz/celebrity-news-gossip/jack-nicholson-you-dont-know-jack/ |date= November 8, 2013 |access-date= January 4, 2026 |publisher= [[CNN]] |title= You don't know Jack (Nicholson) |first= Matt |last= Carey |archive-date= November 14, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131114025419/http://www.cnn.com/2013/11/08/showbiz/celebrity-news-gossip/jack-nicholson-you-dont-know-jack |url-status= live }}</ref> [[Principal photography]] occurred from October 1988 to January 1989 on constructed sets at the backlot of [[Pinewood Studios]] in London.{{sfn|Burton|2000|p=75}}<ref name="Joe">{{cite news|first=Joe|last=Morgenstern|date=April 9, 1989|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/04/09/magazine/tim-burton-batman-and-the-joker.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm|title=Tim Burton, Batman and The Joker|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=October 26, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131220121214/https://www.nytimes.com/1989/04/09/magazine/tim-burton-batman-and-the-joker.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm|archive-date=December 20, 2013|url-status=live}}</ref> After its June 1989 release, ''Batman'' received mostly positive reviews and finished the theatrical run as the year's [[1989 in film#Highest-grossing films|second highest-grossing film]], with a box office take of $400 million.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.metacritic.com/movie/batman | title = Batman (1989): Reviews | work = [[Metacritic]] | access-date = December 27, 2025 | archive-date = March 26, 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130326155043/http://www.metacritic.com/movie/batman | url-status = live }}</ref>{{sfn|Griffin|Masters|1997|p=172–173}} At the [[62nd Academy Awards]], the film won [[Academy Award for Best Art Direction|Best Art Direction]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/1990/B?qt-honorees=1#block-quicktabs-honorees | title = Batman | publisher = [[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] | access-date = September 16, 2017 | archive-date = September 17, 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170917081048/http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/1990/B?qt-honorees=1#block-quicktabs-honorees | url-status = live }}</ref>

===''Batman Returns'' (1992)===
{{main|Batman Returns |l1=''Batman Returns''}}

Studio executives prioritized a sequel to ''Batman'' beginning in late-1989.<ref name="AFICatalog">{{cite web |title=''Batman Returns'' (1992) |url=https://catalog.afi.com/Catalog/moviedetails/59187 |publisher=[[American Film Institute]] |access-date=July 10, 2022 |archive-date=March 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220320185245/https://catalog.afi.com/Catalog/moviedetails/59187 |url-status=live}}</ref> Warner Bros. secured Burton's commitment as director for ''Batman Returns'' in 1991.<ref name="LAtimesJan1991">{{cite web |first=Claudia |last=Puig |title=Movies |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1991-01-10-ca-11005-story.html |website=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=January 10, 1991 |access-date=December 1, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220702224612/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1991-01-10-ca-11005-story.html |archive-date=July 2, 2022 |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref> Burton was reluctant to return for another film because he was cynical about sequels.<ref name="AFICatalog"/> He had also been frustrated with the authority Guber and Peters exerted over the original film, agreeing to ''Batman Returns'' only on the condition of greater independence.{{sfn|Reinhart|2013|p=124}} [[Daniel Waters (screenwriter)|Daniel Waters]] replaced Hamm as screenwriter based on Burton's preference for a writer that had no involvement with ''Batman''.<ref name="DOGBatmanRetro">{{cite web |first=David |last=Crow |title=How ''Batman II'' Became ''Batman Returns'' |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/how-batman-ii-became-batman-returns/ |website=[[Den of Geek]] |date=December 1, 2019 |access-date=December 1, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220617143147/https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/how-batman-ii-became-batman-returns/ |archive-date=June 17, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> Waters developed the script with greater emphasis on the villains—including [[Selina Kyle (Batman Returns)|Catwoman]] ([[Michelle Pfeiffer]]) and the Penguin ([[Danny DeVito]]).<ref name="DOGBatmanRetro"/><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/article/1991/08/09/clawing-catwoman/|title=Big-Game Hunting|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|date=August 9, 1991|access-date=August 14, 2008|first1=Pat H.|last1=Broeske|first2=Anne|last2=Thompson|archive-date=October 13, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081013215612/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,315097,00.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Burton brought on [[Wesley Strick]] for an uncredited rewrite before assigning Waters further script editing duties.<ref name="AFICatalog"/> Keaton reprised his role for a $10 million salary.<ref name="AFICatalog"/><ref name="DOGBatmanRetro"/> Filmmakers shot ''Batman Returns'' from September 1991 to February 1992, and the film was released in theaters that June.<ref name="AFICatalog"/><ref name="LATimestartDate">{{cite web |first=Beth |last=Kleid |title=Movies |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1991-09-03-ca-1951-story.html |website=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=September 3, 1991 |access-date=December 1, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706212058/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1991-09-03-ca-1951-story.html |archive-date=July 6, 2022 |url-status=live|url-access=limited}}</ref><ref name="THRReturnsat25">{{cite web |first=Byron |last=Burton |title=''Batman Returns'' At 25: Stars Reveal Script Cuts, Freezing Sets And Aggressive Penguins |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-returns-at-25-stars-reveal-script-cuts-freezing-sets-aggressive-penguins-1013942/ |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=June 19, 2017 |access-date=December 1, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220624084018/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-returns-at-25-stars-reveal-script-cuts-freezing-sets-aggressive-penguins-1013942/ |archive-date=June 24, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> ''Batman Returns'' polarized critics and saw diminished returns at the box office.<ref name="AFICatalog"/><ref name="greenberg20050508"/>

===''Batman Forever'' (1995)===
{{main|Batman Forever |l1=''Batman Forever''}}
[[File:Val Kilmer Cannes.jpg|thumb|upright|Val Kilmer in 2005]]

To improve their profit-making potential, Warner Bros. developed ''Batman Forever'' with a more family-friendly tone.<ref name="THRReturnsat25"/> The studio did not want to continue the series with Burton and encouraged the filmmaker to seek other projects, though Burton remained involved as an executive producer.<ref name="DOGBatman3">{{cite web |first=David |last=Crow |title=Why Tim Burton's Batman 3 Never Happened |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/why-tim-burtons-batman-3-never-happened/ |website=[[Den of Geek]] |date=August 25, 2019 |access-date=December 1, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220627221212/https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/why-tim-burtons-batman-3-never-happened/ |archive-date=June 27, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=Russo>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/article/1993/10/01/batman-3/ |title=''Batman 3'' |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |date=October 1, 1993 |access-date=August 16, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080921055255/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C308195%2C00.html |archive-date=September 21, 2008 |url-status=live}}</ref> They hired [[Joel Schumacher]] as Burton's replacement, believing he could better realize a film conducive to advertising toys.{{sfn|Proctor|2023|p=221}} The screenplay was conceived by Lee and Janet Scott-Batchler, a husband-and-wife writing team, and [[Akiva Goldsman]].{{sfn|Reinhart|2013|p=155}} Keaton at first supported the changes but in time dropped out, objecting to the script.<ref name="THRReturnsat25"/><ref name=Val>{{cite magazine|last=Gordinier |first=Jeff |url=https://ew.com/article/1994/07/15/val-kilmer-takes-over-batman-forever/ |title=Next at ''Batman'' |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |date=July 15, 1994 |access-date=August 16, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080921055249/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C302969%2C00.html |archive-date=September 21, 2008 |url-status=live}}</ref> 

[[Ethan Hawke]], [[Daniel Day-Lewis]], [[Ralph Fiennes]], and [[Kurt Russell]] were among those considered to play Batman, which eventually went to [[Val Kilmer]].<ref>{{cite news | author = Wiener, Jonah | url = http://www.details.com/celebrities-entertainment/movies-and-tv/201110/ethan-hawke-actor-woman-in-the-fifth | title = Q&A: Ethan Hawke | work = [[Details (magazine)|Details]] | date = October 2011 | access-date = August 7, 2012 | url-status=live | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130809005043/http://www.details.com/celebrities-entertainment/movies-and-tv/201110/ethan-hawke-actor-woman-in-the-fifth | archive-date = August 9, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Jett|title=William Baldwin Talks Batman And "Justice League: Crisis on Two Earths"|work=Batman-on-Film|date=December 16, 2009|url=http://www.batman-on-film.com/dccomics-on-film_JL-CRISIS-ON-TWO-EARTHS_William-Baldwin-talks-Batman_12-16-09.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120226075047/http://www.batman-on-film.com/dccomics-on-film_JL-CRISIS-ON-TWO-EARTHS_William-Baldwin-talks-Batman_12-16-09.html|archive-date=February 26, 2012}}</ref><ref name="LATimesKeaton">{{cite web |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-07-08-ca-13379-story.html |title=It's Still a Riddle Why Keaton Didn't Stay 'Batman Forever'|author=Staff|work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=July 8, 1994 |access-date=April 11, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251012001809/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-07-08-ca-13379-story.html|archive-date=October 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Kilmer came to Schumacher's attention for his work in the Western film ''[[Tombstone (film)|Tombstone]]'' (1993).{{sfn|Kaveney|2007|p=245}} Shooting took place from September 1994 to March 1995,<ref name=Russo/><ref name=thrbf>{{cite web |last=Couch |first=Aaron |title='Batman Forever': The Story Behind the Surprise Hit "Nobody Really Wanted" |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=June 17, 2015 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-forever-story-behind-surprise-802804/|access-date=December 1, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250902112701/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-forever-story-behind-surprise-802804/|archive-date=September 2, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> followed by the theatrical rollout in June 1995.<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Svetkey |first=Benjamin |url=https://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,293237,00.html |title=Holy Happy Set! |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |date=July 12, 1996 |access-date=August 16, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080907051616/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C293237%2C00.html |archive-date=September 7, 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> ''Batman Forever'' finished the year as the [[1995 in film|sixth highest-grossing film]] by amassing $350 million globally, but drew a tepid critical response.<ref name="NYTimeschumachObit">{{cite web |first=Jason |last=Bailey |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/23/movies/joel-schumacher-batman.html |title=Don't Forget That Joel Schumacher Briefly Saved Batman |website=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 23, 2020 | access-date = July 17, 2022 | archive-date = April 18, 2022 | archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20220418232405/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/23/movies/joel-schumacher-batman.html | url-status = live |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Nonetheless, the film received three nominations at the [[68th Academy Awards]].<ref name="Oscars1996">{{cite web |url=http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/1996 |title=The 68th Academy Awards (1996) Nominees and Winners |publisher=[[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] |date=October 5, 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402004458/http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/1996 |archive-date=April 2, 2015 }}</ref>

===''Batman & Robin'' (1997)===
{{main|Batman & Robin (film) |l1=''Batman & Robin'' (film)}}
[[File:George Clooney-4 The Men Who Stare at Goats TIFF09 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|George Clooney in 2009]]

Schumacher was signed as director of ''Batman & Robin'' while ''Batman Forever'' ran in theaters.<ref name=helm>{{cite news|last= Fleming|first= Michael|url= https://variety.com/1997/voices/columns/helmer-s-3rd-at-bat-1117435255/|title= Helmer's 3rd At Bat|work= Variety|date= February 21, 1997|access-date= November 11, 2008 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120212162411/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117435255 |archive-date=February 12, 2012 |url-status=live}}</ref> Goldman and [[Chris O'Donnell]] returned, the former as the film's sole screenwriter.{{sfn|Reinhart|2013|p=177}}<ref>{{cite magazine|last=|first=|url=https://ew.com/movies/chris-odonnell-role-call-ncis-la/ |title=From Men Don't Leave to NCIS: LA, the roles of Chris O'Donnell |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |date=November 7, 2020 |access-date=January 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250418150907/https://ew.com/movies/chris-odonnell-role-call-ncis-la/ |archive-date=April 18, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Kilmer did not reappear, and reports give conflicting accounts about the circumstance of the actor's departure. Schumacher maintained in a 1996 interview that producers "sort of fired" Kilmer because he was volatile on the set of ''Batman Forever''.<ref name=ewVal>{{cite magazine |first=Rebecca |last=Ascher-Walsh |url=https://ew.com/article/1996/05/31/val-kilmer-makes-enemies-hollywood/ |title=Psycho Kilmer |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |date=May 31, 1996 |access-date=November 11, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081201081414/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C292752%2C00.html |archive-date=December 1, 2008 |url-status=live }}</ref> On the other hand, Kilmer cited scheduling conflicts that arose as a result of prior commitments to ''[[Heat (1995 film)|Heat]]'' (1995) and ''[[The Saint (1997 film)|The Saint]]'' (1997).<ref name=valdog>{{cite web |last=Crow |first=David |date=August 30, 2021 |title=Val Kilmer on Batman Forever: 'It's So Bad, It's Almost Good' |url=https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/val-kilmer-batman-forever-so-bad-its-good/ |access-date=February 3, 2024 |website=[[Den of Geek]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250404093409/https://www.denofgeek.com/movies/val-kilmer-batman-forever-so-bad-its-good/|archive-date=April 4, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=shadow>{{cite AV media|people=[[Joel Schumacher]], [[Peter MacGregor-Scott]], [[Chris O'Donnell]], [[Val Kilmer]], [[Uma Thurman]], [[John Glover (actor)|John Glover]]|title=Shadows of the Bat: The Cinematic Saga of the Dark Knight Part 6-Batman Unbound|date=2005|publisher=[[Warner Home Video]]|medium=DVD}}</ref> In his documentary film ''[[Val (film)|Val]]'' (2021), the actor clarified further that the experience working in the Batsuit, which he found cumbersome, influenced his decision to leave.<ref name=valdog/> 

Executive [[Bob Daly]] mentioned [[George Clooney]] in casting discussions, leading to Clooney's hiring based on his performance in ''[[From Dusk till Dawn]]'' (1996) and his likeness to Batman's comic book counterpart.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.eonline.com/photos/28127/stars-who-ve-played-batman|title=Check Out All of the Stars Who've Played Batman|date=March 3, 2022|work=[[E! Online]]|access-date=September 8, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220806223639/https://www.eonline.com/photos/28127/stars-who-ve-played-batman|archive-date=August 6, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> Filming for ''Batman & Robin'' began in September 1996 and finished two weeks ahead of schedule in January 1997,<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Pener |first=Degen |url=https://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,294102,00.html |title=Holy Hearsay |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |date=September 13, 1996 |access-date=November 11, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081011035858/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C294102%2C00.html |archive-date=October 11, 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Anita M.|last=Busch|url=https://www.variety.com/vstory/VR1117433911|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20081218103459/http://www.variety.com/vstory/VR1117433911.html?categoryid=38&cs=1 |url-status=dead|archive-date=December 18, 2008|title=Schumacher on 'Popcorn'|work=Variety|date=January 10, 1997|access-date=November 11, 2008}}</ref><ref name=mike>{{cite news|first1=Michael|last1=Mallory |first2=Mike Jr.|last2=Fleming|url=https://www.variety.com/vstory/VR1117343043|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081218103425/http://www.variety.com/vstory/VR1117343043.html?categoryid=38&cs=1 |url-status=dead|archive-date=December 18, 2008|title=Holy caped caper, IV|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=March 5, 1997|access-date=November 11, 2008}}</ref> and the theatrical release was scheduled in June 1997.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.the-numbers.com/movie/Batman-and-Robin|title=''Batman & Robin'' (1997)|website=[[The Numbers (website)|The Numbers]]|access-date=September 8, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220416104017/https://www.the-numbers.com/movie/Batman-and-Robin|archive-date=April 16, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> ''Batman & Robin'' was a critical and commercial failure and is cited as one of the worst blockbuster films ever made.<ref name="NYTimeschumachObit"/><ref name="TheWrapSequels">{{cite web |first=Adam |last=Chitwood |title=Michael Keaton Explains Why He Didn't Return for ''Batman Forever'' |url=https://www.thewrap.com/why-michael-keaton-didnt-return-for-batman-forever/ |website=[[TheWrap]] |date=January 4, 2022 |access-date=July 17, 2022 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220312203450/https://www.thewrap.com/why-michael-keaton-didnt-return-for-batman-forever/|archive-date=March 12, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Unrealized proposals===

Warner Bros. initiated plans to expand the franchise with several films, including a third Schumacher Batman film commissioned as ''Batman & Robin'' was in production. The studio announced the project with [[Mark Protosevich]] as screenwriter.<ref name=helm/> Schumacher said he planned to revisit a darker storytelling approach, contradicting a ''Los Angeles Times'' piece that claimed he would continue the direction of his other Batman films.<ref name="greenberg20050508">{{cite news |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2005-may-08-ca-batman8-story.html |title=Rescuing Batman |last=Greenberg |first=James |date=May 8, 2005 |work=Los Angeles Times |access-date=May 11, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081202014002/http://articles.latimes.com/2005/may/08/entertainment/ca-batman8 |archive-date=December 2, 2008 |url-status=live |page=E-10}}</ref><ref name=unrealized>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-begins-secret-history-movies-almost-got-made-802032/|title=Before 'Batman Begins': Secret History of the Movies That Almost Got Made|first=Aaron|last=Couch|website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=June 14, 2015|language=en|access-date=March 25, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250808151022/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/batman-begins-secret-history-movies-almost-got-made-802032/|archive-date=August 8, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Protosevich's treatment, a 150-page script named ''Batman Unchained'', revolves around Wayne's efforts to confront figures of his turmoiled past, chiefly [[Scarecrow (DC Comics)|the Scarecrow]], [[Harley Quinn]], and, ultimately, the Joker through a drug-induced hallucination.<ref name=unrealized/><ref>{{cite news|last= Linder|first= Brian|url= https://www.ign.com/articles/2000/07/27/rumblings-from-gotham|title= Rumblings From Gotham|work= [[IGN]]|date= July 27, 2000|access-date= June 11, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250915102254/https://www.ign.com/articles/2000/07/27/rumblings-from-gotham|archive-date=September 15, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> In the film's final scene, Wayne is besieged by a swarm of bats as a symbol of triumph over his fears.<ref name=unrealized/> Warner Bros. cast [[Coolio]] to play Scarecrow, introducing the character in a [[cameo appearance|cameo]] in ''Batman & Robin''.<ref>{{cite web|first=Aaron|last=Couch|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/coolio-was-courted-play-scarecrow-scrapped-batman-robin-sequel-973978|title=Coolio Was Courted to Play Scarecrow in Scrapped 'Batman & Robin' Sequel|work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|date=February 9, 2017|access-date=March 1, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250804120307/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/coolio-was-courted-play-scarecrow-scrapped-batman-robin-sequel-973978/|archive-date=August 4, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Sequel development collapsed after the failure of ''Batman & Robin''.<ref name="greenberg20050508" />

Around the same time, another project titled ''Batman: DarKnight'' was approved by Warner Bros., from a script conceived by novice writers Lee Shapiro and Stephen Wise.<ref name=unrealized/><ref name=Lee/> Shapiro and Wise pitched to the studio on learning that they were contemplating a new direction for Batman.<ref name=unrealized/> Their story was inspired by ''The Dark Knight Returns'', featuring Wayne, disillusioned by crimefighting, retreating from the public, and encouraging Grayson to pursue college.<ref name=Lee/> Grayson has an adversarial relationship with professor Jonathan Crane, civilian persona of Scarecrow, who kidnaps and tortures Grayson in psychological experiments in [[Arkham Asylum]].<ref name=unrealized/> [[Man-Bat]] features in ''DarKnight'' as a secondary villain whose crimes are erroneously blamed on Batman, luring Wayne out of hiding.<ref name=Lee/> By 2001, Warner Bros. brought on Jeff Robinov to commence plans for a reboot, ending all active development of their original Batman series.<ref name=unrealized/><ref name=Lee>{{cite news|first= Bill|last=Ramey|url= http://batman-on-film.com/interview_lshapiro.html|title= Interview: Lee Shapiro|work= Batman-on-Film|date=July 28, 2005|access-date= October 18, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080925094024/http://batman-on-film.com/interview_lshapiro.html|archive-date= September 25, 2008|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Planned relaunch==

At the turn of the millennium, Warner Bros. entered a protracted development period over a Batman film.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/> Three reboot proposals emerged during this time, the earliest being an adaptation of Miller's comic book story arc ''[[Batman: Year One]]'' (1987).<ref name=unrealized/> Schumacher made the suggestion to Warner Bros. in 1998, and within a year, the studio solicited the then-relatively unknown filmmaker [[Darren Aronofsky]] for ideas to approach a remake.<ref name=unrealized/><ref name=Jen>{{cite magazine|last=Jensen |first=Jeff |url=https://ew.com/article/1998/12/04/batman-gets-makeover/ |title=Winging It |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |date=December 4, 1998 |access-date=October 17, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081019113057/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C285969%2C00.html |archive-date=October 19, 2008 |url-status=live}}</ref> According to Aronofsky, the studio was receptive after he quipped, "I'd cast [[Clint Eastwood]] as the Dark Knight, and shoot it in Tokyo, doubling for Gotham City."{{sfn|Hughes|2003|p=199}} He joined as director in 2000.<ref name=Dana>{{cite news|first = Dana|last = Harris|url = https://variety.com/2000/film/news/wb-sends-pi-guy-into-the-bat-cave-1117786714/|title = WB sends ''Pi'' guy into the Bat Cave|work = [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date = September 21, 2000|access-date = December 5, 2025|archive-date = December 12, 2019|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191212213843/https://variety.com/2000/film/news/wb-sends-pi-guy-into-the-bat-cave-1117786714/|url-status = live}}</ref> Aronofsky worked with Miller to write the ''Batman: Year One'' script in their second collaboration; their first work together was an undeveloped screenplay of Miller's multi-issue series ''[[Ronin (DC Comics)|Ronin]]''.{{sfn|Hughes|2003|p=200}} Their script re-conceptualized Batman with working class origins and placed greater emphasis on the character's psychological profile.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/><ref name=unrealized/> [[Christian Bale]] and [[Freddie Prinze, Jr.]] were discussed to star, and Aronofsky campaigned to hire [[Joaquin Phoenix]] against studio intent, but ''Batman: Year One'' never went into production.{{sfn|Hull|2020|p=}}<ref>{{cite news|last1=Godfrey|first1=Alex|last2=Travis|first2=Ben|title=Darren Aronofsky's Batman Fell Apart Because He Wanted Joaquin Phoenix – And The Studio Didn't|url= https://www.empireonline.com/movies/news/darren-aronofsky-batman-joaquin-phoenix-freddie-prinze-jr/|work=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]|date=April 17, 2020|access-date=April 20, 2020}}</ref>  Owing to creative disagreements with Aronofsky and Miller, Warner Bros. abandoned efforts on the project.<ref>{{cite news|last= Tyler|first= Adrienne|title=What Aronofsky's Batman: Year One Movie Looked Like (& Why It Didn't Happen)|url= https://screenrant.com/batman-year-1-darren-aronofsky-plans-cancelled-reason|work= Screenrant|date= May 11, 2020|access-date= March 19, 2024}}</ref>

[[Alan Horn]] succeeded [[Terry Semel]] and [[Bob Daly]] as COO and president of Warner Bros. in 1999. There he implemented plans to relaunch the Batman and Superman franchises as part of a broader measure to increase the studio's output of blockbuster films.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/> The efforts may have been shaped in part by a corporate merger between parent company [[Time Warner]] and [[AOL]] in 2001.{{sfn|Conner|2023|p=96}} Although the idea of a [[crossover (fiction)|crossover]] film portraying Batman and Superman as foes long circulated in the press, it was screenwriter [[Andrew Kevin Walker]] who first brought forward the concept in earnest in August 2001.{{sfn|Darius|2011|p=66–68}} Warner Bros. engaged [[Wolfgang Petersen]] to direct ''Batman Vs. Superman'', who then secured Walker's services to prepare a draft.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/> Goldman was brought on for a rewrite when the studio rejected Walker's draft, but the successive script drew mixed reactions.{{sfn|Darius|2011|p=66–68}} Thereafter Petersen left to make another Warner project, the historical drama ''[[Troy (film)|Troy]]'' (2004), and Horn clashed with producer [[Lorenzo di Bonaventura]] because they had competing visions for the franchises.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/>{{sfn|Darius|2011|p=66–68}} Ultimately, the studio proceeded with plans for solo films and development of ''Batman Vs. Superman'' unraveled.{{sfn|Darius|2011|p=66–68}}

In 2000, Warner Bros. oversaw a live-action adaptation of their animated TV program ''[[Batman Beyond]]''.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/> They commissioned [[Boaz Yakin]] and ''Batman Beyond'' creators [[Paul Dini]] and [[Alan Burnett]] to write a draft, but found the direction unsatisfactory and severed further commitment.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/> Little else is known about the project and filmmakers involved seldom discuss information in interviews with the media.<ref name=unrealized/>

A younger Wayne played by [[Armie Hammer]] was a subject of a [[Justice League]] film known as ''Justice League: Mortal'', which was meant to launch a franchise independent of the mainline Batman films in the late 2000s.<ref name="Kit">{{cite news |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/vine-young-actors-seek-justice-152538 |title=The Vine: Young actors seek ''Justice'' |last=Borys Kit |date=October 15, 2007 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |access-date=March 16, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250303222946/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/vine-young-actors-seek-justice-152538/|url-status=live|archive-date=March 3, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last= Buchanan|first= Kyle|url=https://www.vulture.com/2010/10/armie_hammer_interview.html|title=The Social Network's Armie Hammer Talks Special Effects, Misogyny, and the Downside of Being Tall and Handsome|work=[[New York (magazine)|New York]]|date=October 20, 2010|access-date=September 8, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250317114421/https://www.vulture.com/2010/10/armie_hammer_interview.html|archive-date=March 17, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Several problems beset the production. Warner Bros. suspended filming in the wake of an [[2007–08 Writers Guild of America strike|industrywide labor strike]] by the [[Writers Guild of America]] and again over disputes concerning the studio's request for tax subsidies from the government of Australia, which was denied by the [[Australian Film Commission]].<ref>{{cite news |last= Garrett |first= Diane |url= https://variety.com/2008/film/features/warner-bros-to-serve-justice-in-09-1117981504/ |title= Warner Bros. to serve 'Justice' in '09 |work= [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date= February 26, 2008 |access-date= March 15, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250114224338/https://variety.com/2008/film/features/warner-bros-to-serve-justice-in-09-1117981504/|archive-date=January 14, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Canada">{{cite news |url=https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/unhappy-feet-may-flee-sydney-20080225-gds2gv.html |title=Unhappy feet may flee Sydney |last=Garry Maddox |date=February 25, 2008 |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=February 25, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251219212910/https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/unhappy-feet-may-flee-sydney-20080225-gds2gv.html|url-access=limited |url-status=live|archive-date=December 19, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/mega-movie-refused-rebate-20080319-gds5vh.html |title=Mega movie refused rebate |last=Garry Maddox |date=March 19, 2008 |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=March 19, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251119115035/https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/mega-movie-refused-rebate-20080319-gds5vh.html|archive-date=November 19, 2025|url-access=limited |url-status=live}}</ref> In turn, Warner Bros. relocated the film's [[production office|administrative operations]] to Canada, before cancelling production to mandate solo films of the DC characters, enacted after the release of ''[[The Dark Knight]]'' (2008).<ref name=colliderjl>{{Cite web |last=Farley |first=Lloyd |date=May 25, 2024|title=George Miller's Cancelled Justice League Movie Would've Had a Brainwashed Superman |url=https://collider.com/george-miller-justice-league/ |access-date=December 20, 2025 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |language=en-US|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240607071943/https://collider.com/george-miller-justice-league/|archive-date=June 7, 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=mtvjl>{{cite news|last=Seijas|first=Casey|url=http://splashpage.mtv.com/2009/03/09/exclusive-justice-league-movie-still-a-possibility-says-director-just-not-anytime-soon/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090310173527/http://splashpage.mtv.com/2009/03/09/exclusive-justice-league-movie-still-a-possibility-says-director-just-not-anytime-soon/|url-status=dead|archive-date=March 10, 2009|title=Justice League' Movie Still A Possibility, Says Director... Just Not Anytime Soon|publisher=[[MTV]]|date=March 9, 2009|access-date=September 17, 2014}}</ref>

==''The Dark Knight'' trilogy==
{{see also|Bruce Wayne (Dark Knight trilogy) |l1=Bruce Wayne (''Dark Knight'' trilogy)}}

===''Batman Begins'' (2005)===
{{main|Batman Begins |l1=''Batman Begins''}}
[[File:Christian Bale-7834.jpg|thumb|upright|Christian Bale in 2019]]

[[Christopher Nolan]] was signed to a [[pay-or-play contract]] as director of ''Batman Begins'' in early 2003,<ref name="greenberg20050508"/><ref name=capture>{{cite news|last= Flemming|first= Michael|url= https://variety.com/2003/film/markets-festivals/batman-captures-director-nolan-1117879566/|title= ''Batman'' captures director Nolan|work= Variety|date= January 27, 2003|access-date= September 24, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615093935/https://variety.com/2003/film/markets-festivals/batman-captures-director-nolan-1117879566/|archive-date=June 15, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> after approaching Warner Bros. with the idea of making a Batman film centered on the character's origins.<ref name="bb-latimes">{{Cite news |last=Greenberg |first=James |date=May 8, 2005 |title=Rescuing Batman |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2005-may-08-ca-batman8-story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191217132113/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2005-may-08-ca-batman8-story.html |archive-date= December 17, 2019 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref> At the time, the studio wanted to reconcile relations with the filmmaker after Petersen took his place as ''Troy'' director.<ref name="NolanTroyConfirm">{{Cite magazine |last=Sharf |first=Zack |date=November 21, 2025 |title=Christopher Nolan Was Hired to Direct ''Troy'' More Than 20 Years Before ''The Odyssey''; He Says the Studio Backtracked and Offered ''Batman Begins'' as 'Consolation Prize' |url=https://variety.com/2025/film/news/christopher-nolan-hired-troy-director-the-odyssey-1236588697/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251122064513/https://variety.com/2025/film/news/christopher-nolan-hired-troy-director-the-odyssey-1236588697/ |archive-date=November 22, 2025 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref>  Nolan said he aimed to develop a more realistic, grittier film setting to differentiate ''Batman Begins'' from Warner's original Batman movies.<ref name="beautiful">{{cite news|first1=Marc|last1=Graser |first2=Cathy|last2=Dunkley |url= https://variety.com/2004/biz/news/the-bat-and-the-beautiful-1117899714/|title= The bat and the beautiful|work= [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date= February 8, 2004|access-date= November 2, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250804030139/https://variety.com/2004/biz/news/the-bat-and-the-beautiful-1117899714/|archive-date=August 4, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> This encompassed the creation of an updated [[Batmobile]] and an all-black [[Batsuit]] designed for more agile movement.{{sfn|Reinhart|2013|p=213}}<ref name="batsuitbb">{{cite web|url=http://www2.warnerbros.com/batmanbegins/productionnotes/|title=''Batman Begins'' Production Notes – The Batsuit & Gadgetry|publisher=[[Warner Bros.]]|access-date=December 22, 2025| archive-date=October 28, 2006| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061028060305/http://www.cinemareview.com/production.asp?prodid=3003}}</ref> 

Nolan and [[David S. Goyer]] produced the film's completed script.<ref name="beginsending"/> Management cast Bale, under Nolan's belief that he exuded "exactly the balance of darkness and light" they desired for the character.<ref name="greenberg20050508"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/3102536.stm|title=Actor Bale lands Batman role|publisher=[[BBC News]]|date=September 13, 2003|access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081130111111/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/3102536.stm|archive-date=November 30, 2008|url-status=live}}</ref> To prepare for the role, Bale was given martial arts training, regained the weight he lost for ''[[The Machinist]]'' (2004), and increased his muscle mass, weighing about {{convert|220|lb|kg}}.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Evans|first1=Matthew|url=https://www.menshealth.com/uk/fitness/a758307/5-of-christian-bales-craziest-body-transformations/|title=Christian Bale's 5 Craziest Body Transformations|work=[[Men's Health]]|date=February 11, 2017|access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210124050157/https://www.menshealth.com/uk/fitness/a758307/5-of-christian-bales-craziest-body-transformations/|archive-date=January 24, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Dibin|first1=Emma|url=https://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/film/news/a9814/why-christian-bale-style-yo-yo-dieting-is-terrible-for-youy/|title=Why Christian Bale-Style Yo-Yo Dieting Is Terrible For You|work=[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]|url-access=limited|date=April 14, 2016|access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112035108/https://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/film/news/a9814/why-christian-bale-style-yo-yo-dieting-is-terrible-for-youy/|archive-date=November 12, 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> The filmmaking crew spent 2004 shooting ''Batman Begins'' in Iceland, the United Kingdom and Chicago, the lattermost within a three-week period.<ref>{{cite web |first=Cam'ron |last=Hardy |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2025/08/19/batman-begins-chicago-20-years/ |title=It's been 20 years since 'Batman Begins' and 'Dark Knight' changed how the world saw Chicago |website=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=August 19, 2025 | access-date = December 22, 2025 | archive-date = December 22, 2025 | archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20251222214805/https://www.chicagotribune.com/2025/08/19/batman-begins-chicago-20-years/ | url-status = live |url-access=subscription}}</ref> They relied on [[miniature effect]]s and traditional [[stunt]]s during the production, using [[computer-generated imagery]] (CGI) only sparingly.{{sfn|Nathan|2022|p=69}} Despite a poor box office prognosis, the film was released in June 2005 to improved results, grossing $375.4 million worldwide.<ref name="Kelly-2005">{{Cite news |last=Kelly |first=Kate |date=June 20, 2005 |title='Batman Begins' Fails to Break Movie Industry's Losing Streak |language=en-US |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB111923250139263805 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |issn=0099-9660}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2005 Worldwide Box Office |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2005/?ref_=bo_cso_table_1 |publisher=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=December 22, 2025 |archive-date=July 7, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190707054520/https://www.boxofficemojo.com/yearly/chart/?view2=worldwide&yr=2005&p=.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Reviews from critics were very positive, and ''Batman Begins'' became a candidate for [[Academy Award for Best Cinematography|Best Cinematography]] at the [[78th Academy Awards]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Batman Begins''|date=June 15, 2005 |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/batman_begins/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180115152611/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/batman_begins/ |archive-date=January 15, 2018 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref><ref name="Oscars2006">{{Cite news|url=http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/2006 |title=The 78th Academy Awards (2006) Nominees and Winners |access-date=December 22, 2025 |publisher=[[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] (AMPAS) |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141109220946/http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/2006 |archive-date=November 9, 2014 }}</ref>

===''The Dark Knight'' (2008)===
{{main|The Dark Knight |l1=''The Dark Knight''}}

Nolan did not plan to make a sequel, but nevertheless brainstormed ideas with Goyer during the filming of ''Batman Begins''.<ref name="EmpireHowtheJokerwasmade">{{cite web|first=Dan |last=Jolin |title=The Making of the Joker |url=http://www.empireonline.com/features/heath-ledger-joker |website=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]] |date=2012 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |archive-date=December 9, 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131209000355/http://www.empireonline.com/features/heath-ledger-joker|url-status=dead}}</ref> The men worked together to outline ''The Dark Knight''{{'s}} essential plot points for three months.<ref name="VarietyReviewBlog">{{cite web|first=Anne |last=Thompson |author-link=Anne Thompson (film journalist) |title=''Dark Knight'' Review: Nolan Talks Sequel Inflation |url=http://weblogs.variety.com/thompsononhollywood/2008/07/dark-knight-rev.html |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=July 6, 2008 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |archive-date=July 10, 2008 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080710052656/http://weblogs.variety.com/thompsononhollywood/2008/07/dark-knight-rev.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Nolan next assisted his brother [[Jonathan Nolan|Jonathan]] with development of the script, starting with a draft screenplay finished in six months.<ref name="VarietyReviewBlog"/> The brothers spent another six months collaborating on the final script.<ref name="VarietyReviewBlog"/> Filmmakers again redesigned the Batsuit to make it more comfortable to wear.<ref name="EWBatsuit">{{cite magazine|first=Jeff |last=Jensen |title=Batman's New Suit |url=http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C20042739%2C00.html |magazine= [[Entertainment Weekly]]|date=June 18, 2007 |access-date=December 22, 2025 |archive-date= September 14, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100914092558/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C20042739%2C00.html |url-status=dead}}</ref>{{sfn|Duncan Jesser|Pourroy|2012|p=113}} Bale reprised his role as Batman, performing many of his own stunts.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Celizic|first1=Mike|url=https://www.today.com/popculture/christian-bale-ledger-had-wonderful-time-joker-wbna25673502|title=Christian Bale: Ledger had 'wonderful time' as Joker|work=[[Today (American TV program)|Today]]|date=July 14, 2008|access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201123224608/https://www.today.com/popculture/christian-bale-ledger-had-wonderful-time-joker-wbna25673502|archive-date=November 23, 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> The film story sees Batman battling his arch-nemesis the [[Joker (The Dark Knight)|Joker]] ([[Heath Ledger]]), who obstructs efforts to control organized crime by his newly-forged alliance with district attorney [[Harvey Dent]] ([[Aaron Eckhart]]) and police lieutenant [[Commissioner Gordon|James Gordon]] ([[Gary Oldman]]). 

''The Dark Knight'' was shot on a 127-day schedule from April to November 2007, and opened to widespread critical acclaim in July 2008.{{sfn|Duncan Jesser|Pourroy|2012|p=159–160, 189}}<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.metacritic.com/movie/the-dark-knight|title=''The Dark Knight'' Reviews |website=[[Metacritic]]|access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140209142618/http://www.metacritic.com/movie/the-dark-knight|archive-date=February 9, 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> It broke numerous box office records, becoming the [[2008 in film|highest-grossing film of 2008]] and exceeding $1&nbsp;billion by February 2009.{{sfn|Duncan Jesser|Pourroy|2012|p=49}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.eonline.com/uberblog/b101032__lt_i_gt_The_Dark_Knight_lt__i_gt__Breaks_a_Billion.html|title=''The Dark Knight'' breaks a billion|date=February 20, 2009 |website=[[E!]] |access-date=December 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110305194437/http://www.eonline.com/uberblog/b101032__lt_i_gt_The_Dark_Knight_lt__i_gt__Breaks_a_Billion.html|archive-date=March 5, 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> Near the end of its global rollout, the film entered the [[81st Academy Awards]] season as a frontrunner with eight nominations, winning two.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/2009 |title=The 81st Academy Awards (2009) Nominees and Winners |access-date=December 22, 2025 |publisher=[[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]]|url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141110111206/http://www.oscars.org/oscars/ceremonies/2009 |archive-date=November 10, 2014 }}</ref> Ledger's posthumous win for [[Academy Award for Best Supporting Actor|Best Supporting Actor]] made ''The Dark Knight'' the first comic book film to win an Academy Award for acting.<ref name="IGNOscar">{{cite web|first=William |last=Bibbiani |title= The History Of Comic Book Movies at the Oscars|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/the-history-of-comic-book-movies-at-the-oscars |website= [[IGN]]|date= February 8, 2022|access-date=December 22, 2025 |archive-date=May 31, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531032028/https://www.ign.com/articles/the-history-of-comic-book-movies-at-the-oscars |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2020, the United States [[Library of Congress]] selected the film for preservation in the [[National Film Registry]].<ref name="LOCTHR">{{cite web | url = https://variety.com/2020/film/news/dark-knight-shrek-grease-blues-brothers-national-film-registry-1234852610/ | title = ''Dark Knight'', ''Shrek'', ''Grease'', ''Blues Brothers'' Added To National Film Registry | first = Dave | last = McNary|author-link=Dave McNary | date = December 14, 2020 | access-date = January 6, 2026 | website= [[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] | archive-date = December 14, 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201214080223/https://variety.com/2020/film/news/dark-knight-shrek-grease-blues-brothers-national-film-registry-1234852610/ | url-status = live }}</ref>

===''The Dark Knight Rises'' (2012)===
{{main|The Dark Knight Rises |l1=''The Dark Knight Rises''}}

After initial hesitation, Nolan returned to direct ''The Dark Knight Rises'', and, with his brother and Goyer, conceived a story he believed would conclude the trilogy on a satisfying note.{{sfn|Nathan|2022|pp=138–139}}<ref>{{cite news |url=http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/herocomplex/2010/03/christopher-nolan-takes-flight-with-superman-we-have-a-fantastic-story-1.html |title=Christopher Nolan takes flight with Superman: 'We have a fantastic story' [UPDATED] |last=Boucher |first=Geoff |date=March 10, 2010 |work=Los Angeles Times |access-date=July 5, 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100704194608/http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/herocomplex/2010/03/christopher-nolan-takes-flight-with-superman-we-have-a-fantastic-story-1.html |archive-date= July 4, 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref> He contemplated story and character ideas with Goyer before tasking Jonathan with the scriptwriting.{{sfn|Duncan Jesser|Pourroy|2012|p=51}} Warner Bros. proposed a character similar to Ledger's Joker as the film's primary villain, but Nolan picked [[Bane (DC Comics)|Bane]] ([[Tom Hardy]]), favoring a physically imposing figure as antagonist.<ref name="beginsending">{{cite web |last=Jolin |first=Dan |date=August 19, 2020 |title=The Dark Knight Trilogy: The Complete Story Of Christopher Nolan's Batman Films |url=https://www.empireonline.com/movies/features/dark-knight-trilogy-complete-story-of-christopher-nolan-batman/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200820204118/https://www.empireonline.com/movies/features/dark-knight-trilogy-complete-story-of-christopher-nolan-batman/ |archive-date=August 20, 2020 |access-date=December 26, 2025|website=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]}}</ref><ref name=Banechoice>{{cite news|url=http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2011/12/12/dark-knight-rises-christopher-nolan-opens-up-about-bane-choice/|title='Dark Knight Rises': Christopher Nolan opens up about Bane choice|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|date=December 12, 2011|access-date=January 8, 2012|archive-date=January 7, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120107222447/http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2011/12/12/dark-knight-rises-christopher-nolan-opens-up-about-bane-choice/|url-status=live}}</ref> The director cited ''[[Metropolis (1927)|Metropolis]]'' (1927), ''[[Doctor Zhivago (film)|Doctor Zhivago]]'' (1965), ''[[The Battle of Algiers]]'' (1966), ''[[Prince of the City]]'' (1981), and ''[[Blade Runner]]'' (1982) as major influences on ''The Dark Knight Rises''{{'s}} artistic direction.<ref>{{cite news|last= Vejvoda|first= Jim|url= https://www.ign.com/articles/2012/07/30/chris-nolans-dark-knight-rises-movie-influences|title= Chris Nolan's Dark Knight Rises Movie Influences|work= [[IGN]]|date= July 30, 2012|access-date= December 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250218082742/https://www.ign.com/articles/2012/07/30/chris-nolans-dark-knight-rises-movie-influences|archive-date=February 18, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> One of Nolan's main goals was to shoot the film with [[IMAX]] cameras as he wanted visual uniformity between ''The Dark Knight'' projects.<ref name="release">{{cite news |url=http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2010/10/27/nolan-dark-knight-rises-finds-the-future-in-imax-not-3d/ |title=Nolan: 'Dark Knight Rises' finds the future in IMAX, not 3-D |last=Boucher |first=Geoff |date=October 27, 2010 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |access-date=October 28, 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20101030034239/http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2010/10/27/nolan-dark-knight-rises-finds-the-future-in-imax-not-3d/ |archive-date= October 30, 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Production lasted from May to November 2011, and ''The Dark Knight Rises'' debuted in North American theaters in July 2012.<ref>{{cite web|first=Phil|last=de Semlyen|url=https://www.empireonline.com/news/story.asp?NID=29533|title=Exclusive: The Dark Knight Rises In May|date=November 19, 2010|work=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]|access-date=December 26, 2025|archive-date=October 25, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111025174150/http://www.empireonline.com/news/story.asp?nid=29533|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=McNary|first=Dave|url=https://variety.com/2010/digital/markets-festivals/batman-sets-date-1118018621/|title=Batman sets date|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=May 3, 2010|access-date=December 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250425210548/https://variety.com/2010/digital/markets-festivals/batman-sets-date-1118018621/|archive-date=April 25, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> The film eventually surpassed ''The Dark Knight''{{'s}} box office gross and drew highly positive reviews from critics.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/title/tt1345836/?ref_=bo_rl_ti |title=The Dark Knight Rises (2012) |website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=December 26, 2025 |archive-date=April 5, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170405035530/http://www.boxofficemojo.com/movies/?id=batman3.htm |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.metacritic.com/movie/the-dark-knight-rises|title=''The Dark Knight Rises'' Reviews |website=[[Metacritic]]|access-date=December 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250516012954/https://www.metacritic.com/movie/the-dark-knight-rises/|archive-date=May 16, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

